---
title: Masks & layers
description: Build a mask from radial, gradient and brush layers, hang adjustments off it, and grade one part of a frame without touching the rest.
---

A mask is how you adjust part of a frame rather than all of it — lift a subject out of the
background, hold a sky down, warm one corner. In Latent a mask is not a single shape: it is a
stack of layers that combine into one selection, and the adjustments hang off that selection.

## Building a mask

**Create new mask** starts one. The tool row under **Add to this mask** adds a layer to it —
radial gradients, linear gradients and brush strokes among them. Each layer you add appears under
**Layers of this mask**, and the panel reports what the combination covers, as a percentage of the
frame.

Per layer you get:

- **Feather** — how hard the edge falls off
- **Opacity** — how much of the layer counts
- Duplicate and delete
- A visibility toggle, so you can see the mask with a layer taken out

The mask as a whole has its own **Opacity** on top of that, which scales everything hanging off
it. While you are working on a mask, the covered region is shown as a red overlay on the canvas;
the eye control at the top of the Masks panel turns that overlay off so you can judge the result
instead of the selection.

Every frame also has a **Whole photo** entry — the global adjustments from
[Develop](/docs/latent/develop), listed alongside the masks so the whole stack reads in one place.

## Adjustments in a mask

An empty mask does nothing. Move a slider in the Edit panel while a mask is selected and that
adjustment lands under **Adjustments in this mask** — exposure, clarity, and the rest of the
develop controls, scoped to the selection.

## The layer stack

The **Layers** panel gives you the whole edit as an ordered stack: global adjustments and masks
together, each with its own opacity, visibility toggle, duplicate and delete. Entries scoped to
the whole frame are marked as such; a mask expands to show the adjustments it carries. Layers can
be reordered, which changes the order they are applied in.

That ordering is the thing to reach for when two adjustments fight each other — it is usually
cheaper to move one than to compensate with a third.
